            <ArticleTitle>Effects of intrathecally administered dexmedetomidine, MPV-2426 and tizanidine on EMG in rats.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>347-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">When administered intrathecally, alpha-2 adrenergic agonists produce spinally mediated antinociception, but also rapidly redistribute to supraspinal sites. This investigation the compared EMG effects of intrathecally administered dexmedetomidine, MPV-2426 (fadolmidine), and tizanidine in Sprague-Dawley rats, which has not been previously described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We studied electromyographic (EMG) responses of the head and gastrocnemius muscles, antinociception using the tail-flick test, and sedation by using observer assessment. Saline, dexmedetomidine (0.5 microg, 2.5 microg and 12.5 microg), MPV-2426 (2 microg, 10 microg and 50 microg) and tizanidine (2 microg, 10 microg and 50 microg) were administered intrathecally.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Tizanidine 50 microg, MPV-2426 10 microg and 50 microg, and dexmedetomidine 2.5 microg and 12.5 microg, decreased EMG activity (P &lt; 0.005). Dexmedetomidine 12.5 microg, MPV-2426 50 microg, and tizanidine 10 microg and 50 microg increased tail-flick latencies (P &lt; 0.01). Dexmedetomidine alone significantly increased the magnitude of observer-assessed sedation (P &lt; 0.0001).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">We conclude that in rats, intrathecally administered dexmedetomidine, MPV-2426 and tizanidine have dose-dependent effects on EMG. At antinociceptive doses, the EMG effects of these three alpha-2 adrenergic agonists differ (dexmedetomidine &gt; MPV-2426 &gt; tizanidine).</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
